theory CompliantRecovery
begin

/*
================================================================================
  SEALING A LINE BEFORE LEAVING IT
================================================================================

  WHICH TREE THIS IS.  `compliant/`.  `wire-only/recovery.spthy` proves what a
  patron can check about recovery evidence.  This theory is about an ORDER OF
  OPERATIONS the subject's own client must observe, which no one else can
  verify it observed.

  THE OBLIGATION.  `light-client-requirements.md`:

    "On suspected key compromise, SEAL BEFORE YOU TAKE A SERIES REISSUE.  Set
     the counter of the series you are leaving to its maximum, then take the
     reissue naming that value, and do it for every patron relationship you
     hold.  Nothing the holder of your old key signs can then supersede your
     last record in that line.  ACT ON SUSPICION: against a counterparty that
     holds no chain this is a race, and the thief wins it by reaching them
     first."

  and `wire-format.md` 4.6 supplies the arithmetic the promise rests on:

    "A node that believes its key is compromised sets the counter it is
     leaving to the MAXIMUM, then reissues naming that value -- after which
     nothing the thief signs can supersede it, since a strictly greater
     counter does not exist and an equal one [does not supersede]."

  WHAT IS ACTUALLY BEING CHECKED.  Not that the arithmetic is right -- it is,
  trivially.  What is worth checking is that the ORDER is load-bearing and
  that the model says so: seal-then-reissue holds the line, and the reverse
  order does not.  A client that reissues first and seals afterwards leaves a
  window, and the design's "act on suspicion" is a statement about that
  window.  Both traces are asserted below, because a theory that proved only
  the good order would not show that the order is what did the work.

  THE RACE IS NOT MODELLED AWAY.  "Against a counterparty that holds no chain
  this is a race, and the thief wins it by reaching them first."  A holder
  with no chain cannot rank the two records at all, and
  `thief_wins_against_a_chainless_counterparty` keeps that trace reachable.
  It is an accepted residual, not a gap, and a model that lost it would be
  claiming more than the design does.
*/

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  COUNTERS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Counters are symbolic.  The only facts needed are the two `wire-format.md`
  4.6 relies on: nothing is strictly greater than the maximum, and equality
  does not supersede.  Both are restrictions on a `StrictlyGreater` action
  rather than arithmetic, which keeps the search small and models exactly the
  property the text invokes -- no more.
*/

restriction NothingExceedsTheMaximum:
  "All c #i. StrictlyGreater(c, 'max') @ #i ==> F"

restriction EqualDoesNotSupersede:
  "All c #i. StrictlyGreater(c, c) @ #i ==> F"

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE SUBJECT'S TWO ORDERS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*/

/*  THE LINE IS THE UNIT, not the subject.  A node holds one series per
    patron relationship and the requirement says to seal "for EVERY patron
    relationship you hold" -- so the promise is made per line, and a subject
    sealing one line says nothing about another.  Modelling it per subject
    made the main lemma false for an uninteresting reason: one `$S` could run
    the compliant order on one line and the careless order on another, and
    the thief's success on the second falsified a claim about the first.  */
rule Suspects_Compromise:
    [ Fr(~ln) ]
  --[ Suspects($S, ~ln) ]->
    [ Line($S, ~ln, 'open') ]

/*  THE COMPLIANT ORDER.  Seal the line being left at the maximum counter,
    THEN take the reissue.  The seal is the last thing the old key does
    (`light-client-requirements.md`, design 9.0), which is why it consumes
    the open line and nothing reopens it.                                   */
rule Seal_Then_Reissue:
    [ Line($S, ln, 'open') ]
  --[ Sealed($S, ln, 'max'), SealedBeforeReissue($S, ln) ]->
    [ Line($S, ln, 'sealed'), !LastRecord($S, ln, 'max') ]

rule Reissue_After_Sealing:
    [ Line($S, ln, 'sealed') ]
  --[ Reissued($S, ln), ReissuedInOrder($S, ln) ]->
    [ ]

/*  THE OTHER ORDER, present because the property is about which one holds.
    The client reissues while the old line is still open, and seals later --
    or not at all.  The line's last record is then whatever counter it had
    reached, not the maximum.                                               */
rule Reissue_Then_Seal:
    [ Line($S, ln, 'open'), Fr(~c) ]
  --[ Reissued($S, ln), ReissuedOutOfOrder($S, ln) ]->
    [ !LastRecord($S, ln, ~c) ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE THIEF
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The thief holds the old key and can sign a record in the abandoned line at
  any counter it likes.  Superseding the subject's last record requires a
  STRICTLY GREATER counter, which is where the two restrictions above bite.
*/

rule Thief_Supersedes:
    [ !LastRecord($S, ln, c), In(c2) ]
  --[ ThiefSuperseded($S, ln, c2)
    , StrictlyGreater(c2, c) ]->
    [ ]

/*  A counterparty that HOLDS THE CHAIN rejects the abandoned series outright
    -- `wire-format.md` 4.6: it "knows the series was abandoned and MUST
    reject records in it whatever their counter".  A counterparty holding no
    chain has no basis to rank and takes what reaches it first.             */
rule Chainless_Counterparty_Accepts:
    [ In(<'record', $S, c>) ]
  --[ AcceptedWithoutAChain($S, c) ]->
    [ ]

/*
================================================================================
  PROPERTIES
================================================================================
*/

/*  THE OBLIGATION.  A subject that sealed before reissuing has nothing the
    thief can supersede.  This is the promise in the client requirement,
    stated as a trace property.                                             */
lemma sealing_first_leaves_nothing_to_supersede:
  all-traces
  "All S ln c #i #j.
      SealedBeforeReissue(S, ln) @ #i & ThiefSuperseded(S, ln, c) @ #j
        ==> F"

/*  THE ORDER IS WHAT DID IT.  Reissue first and the thief can supersede --
    so the requirement's word "before" is load-bearing and not stylistic.
    If this ever falsifies, the model has stopped distinguishing the two
    orders and the lemma above would hold for the wrong reason.             */
lemma reissuing_first_leaves_a_window:
  exists-trace
  "Ex S ln c #i #j.
      ReissuedOutOfOrder(S, ln) @ #i
    & ThiefSuperseded(S, ln, c) @ #j
    & #i < #j"

/*  ANTI-VACUITY: the compliant path completes.                             */
lemma the_compliant_order_completes:
  exists-trace
  "Ex S ln #i #j.
      SealedBeforeReissue(S, ln) @ #i
    & ReissuedInOrder(S, ln) @ #j
    & #i < #j"

/*  THE ACCEPTED RESIDUAL.  Sealing protects a counterparty that can rank
    records.  One holding no chain cannot, and "the thief wins it by reaching
    them first."  Keeping this reachable is what stops the theory from
    reading as though sealing were a complete defence.                      */
lemma thief_wins_against_a_chainless_counterparty:
  exists-trace
  "Ex S ln c #i #j.
      SealedBeforeReissue(S, ln) @ #i
    & AcceptedWithoutAChain(S, c) @ #j"


/*
================================================================================
  THE RECOVERY MEETING -- what a conforming verifier will not sign
================================================================================

  WHY THIS IS HERE AND NOT IN `wire-only/`.  Three properties used to live in
  `wire-only/recovery.spthy` and did not belong there.  `wire-format.md` 4.1
  says the human evidence carries no guarantee: a verifier "can be mistaken
  or lying and nothing checks it".  A theory that requires a meeting before V
  may use V's OWN uncompromised key is assuming client conformity, and no
  validator can check conformity.

  KEY COMPROMISE IS NOT DISHONESTY.  The old model carved the residual out
  under `Compromised(V)`, which means someone stole V's key.  A verifier who
  lies with its own key needs no theft, so the carve-out was naming the wrong
  capability and the theorem read stronger than it was.  Here it is
  `SkippedTheMeeting`, named for what it is.

  design 19 already registers the composition -- a stolen device plus a
  colluding counterparty producing false recovery evidence -- as an accepted
  residual, so nothing below is a new weakness.  It is the same residual,
  stated where it can be seen.
*/

rule Prior_Counterparty:
    [ ]
  --[ PriorCounterparty($V, $S) ]->
    [ !Met($V, $S) ]

/*  The successor is minted AT the meeting and travels the direct channel --
    design 7.1's optical channel "carries key exchange" -- never the network. */
rule Recovery_Meeting:
    [ !Met($V, $S), Fr(~nk) ]
  --[ MeetingHappened($V, $S, ~nk) ]->
    [ AtMeeting($V, $S, ~nk), Out(~nk) ]
    // `Out` because the SUBJECT leaves the meeting holding its own successor
    // and must name it in the old-key proof.  Without it the two factors can
    // never name one key and no honest recovery completes --
    // `a_conforming_recovery_completes` reported *falsified, no trace found*
    // the first time this rule was written, which is what that lemma is for.

rule Recognise_Compliant:
    [ AtMeeting($V, $S, nk) ]
  --[ RecognisedFor($V, $S, nk) ]->
    [ Recognition($V, $S, nk) ]

/*  AND THE VERIFIER THAT LIES.  A genuine prior counterparty, its own key,
    a successor off the network, no meeting.                                */
rule Recognise_Without_Meeting:
    [ !Met($V, $S), In(nk) ]
  --[ RecognisedFor($V, $S, nk), SkippedTheMeeting($V) ]->
    [ Recognition($V, $S, nk) ]

rule Old_Key_Proof:
    [ In(nk) ]
  --[ OldKeyProof($S, nk, $P) ]->
    [ Proof($S, nk, $P) ]

rule Patron_Accept_Recovery:
    [ Proof($S, nk, $P), Recognition($V, $S, nk) ]
  --[ RecoveryAccepted($P, $S, nk), ReliedOn($P, $V, $S) ]->
    [ ]

/*  THE CONFORMANCE THEOREM.  Where verifiers keep the discipline, an accepted
    recovery implies a meeting.  The honest form of what
    `recovery_requires_a_meeting` was reaching for.                         */
lemma recovery_between_conforming_parties_requires_a_meeting:
  all-traces
  "All P S nk #i.
      RecoveryAccepted(P, S, nk) @ #i
      & not(Ex V #s. SkippedTheMeeting(V) @ #s)
        ==> Ex V #m. MeetingHappened(V, S, nk) @ #m & #m < #i"

/*  And that a conforming recognition names the key THAT meeting exchanged --
    not one supplied to the verifier some other way.                        */
lemma a_conforming_recognition_names_the_meeting_key:
  all-traces
  "All V S nk #b.
      RecognisedFor(V, S, nk) @ #b
      & not(Ex X #s. SkippedTheMeeting(X) @ #s)
        ==> Ex #m. MeetingHappened(V, S, nk) @ #m & #m < #b"

/*  ANTI-VACUITY: a conforming recovery completes.                          */
lemma a_conforming_recovery_completes:
  exists-trace
  "Ex P S nk #i.
      RecoveryAccepted(P, S, nk) @ #i
    & not(Ex V #s. SkippedTheMeeting(V) @ #s)"

/*  THE RESIDUAL, KEPT REACHABLE.  A thief with the old key plus one prior
    counterparty willing to lie completes a recovery with no meeting behind
    it, using nothing but their own keys.  design 19 accepts this; a model in
    which it became unreachable would claim more than the design does.     */
lemma a_lying_verifier_completes_a_recovery_without_a_meeting:
  exists-trace
  "Ex P S nk #i.
      RecoveryAccepted(P, S, nk) @ #i
    & not(Ex V #m. MeetingHappened(V, S, nk) @ #m)"


end
